8
00:00:21.337 --> 00:00:34.009
How one deployment of the grocery platform safely serves many isolated customer organizations (tenants), data isolation, performance isolation, tiered plans, and tenant lifecycle.

9
00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:47.367
Two labs run against real PostgreSQL 16 (isolation models + Row-Level Security) and two against real Redis and a deterministic scheduler (rate limiting, noisy-neighbor fairness).

10
00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:50.935
L1: data isolation models.

11
00:00:51.215 --> 00:01:05.274
On real PostgreSQL, a shared table leaked all 9 rows across 3 tenants the moment a query forgot WHERE tenantid=?; schema-per-tenant and database-per-tenant made that same forgotten filter harmless.

12
00:01:05.554 --> 00:01:06.365
L2:

13
00:01:06.645 --> 00:01:07.989
Row-Level Security.

14
00:01:08.269 --> 00:01:24.311
A real RLS policy scoped every query to the connection's tenant: the L1 leak query returned only 3 rows, a cross-tenant UPDATE hit 0 rows, and with no tenant set it failed closed (0 rows), the leak became impossible.

15
00:01:25.661 --> 00:01:27.283
L3: noisy neighbor.

16
00:01:27.563 --> 00:01:40.640
A 300-task burst from one tenant pushed quiet tenants' wait to ~224 ticks under FIFO; per-tenant fair queuing dropped it to ~0 while the noisy tenant simply drained its own burst.

17
00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.691
L4: tiered rate limiting.

18
00:01:42.971 --> 00:01:57.669
A real Redis atomic token bucket let the free tenant through 5 or 30, pro 20 or 30, enterprise 30 or 30, metered each tenant's served requests for billing, and refilled ~2 tokens in 4 s for the free plan.

19
00:01:57.949 --> 00:01:58.931
Reading time:

20
00:01:59.211 --> 00:02:01.131
120 min + code.

21
00:02:02.481 --> 00:02:03.334
Part 1.

22
00:02:03.614 --> 00:02:05.534
Introduction and Why It Matters.

23
00:02:06.784 --> 00:02:09.941
A story: the report that showed another company's orders.

24
00:02:10.221 --> 00:02:15.043
The grocery platform started as a single-tenant system for one retail chain.

25
00:02:15.323 --> 00:02:23.408
It grew into a SaaS product: dozens of independent retail brands (tenants) ran their storefronts on one shared deployment.

26
00:02:23.688 --> 00:02:27.421
The team added a tenantid column to every table and moved on.

27
00:02:27.701 --> 00:02:36.875
Then, on a Monday, a brand manager at Acme Grocers opened the weekly sales report and saw line items from Globex Foods, a competitor.

28
00:02:37.155 --> 00:02:44.813
The report query had been written months earlier, before multi-tenancy, and it simply never got a WHERE tenantid =? clause.

29
00:02:45.093 --> 00:02:48.784
One forgotten filter, and a customer saw a competitor's revenue.

30
00:02:49.064 --> 00:02:52.413
The incident review found the multi-tenancy was skin-deep:

31
00:02:53.763 --> 00:02:57.966
Isolation depended on every query remembering the tenant filter.

32
00:02:58.246 --> 00:03:01.979
Hundreds of queries, one human mistake away from a breach each.

33
00:03:02.259 --> 00:03:04.606
There was no backstop (L1, L2).

34
00:03:04.886 --> 00:03:09.174
A batch import from one large tenant regularly slowed everyone down.

35
00:03:09.454 --> 00:03:17.518
One tenant's nightly 2-million-row load saturated the shared connection pool; small tenants' checkout latency tripled every night.

36
00:03:17.798 --> 00:03:20.145
No performance isolation (L3).

37
00:03:21.495 --> 00:03:23.329
Every tenant got the same limits.

38
00:03:23.609 --> 00:03:32.783
A free-trial tenant hammering the API with a scraper degraded paying customers, because there were no per-tenant rate limits or plans (L4).

39
00:03:33.063 --> 00:03:35.260
Offboarding was manual and incomplete.

40
00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:45.737
When a tenant churned, deleting only their data across 40 shared tables was a risky hand-written script; nobody was confident it was complete (a compliance problem).

41
00:03:46.017 --> 00:03:47.553
Cost per tenant was unknown.

42
00:03:47.833 --> 00:03:54.404
Finance couldn't say whether the free tier was subsidized by whom, because nothing attributed resource use to tenants.

43
00:03:55.754 --> 00:04:11.221
The rebuild made tenancy a first-class property of the platform: isolation enforced by the database (not by discipline), performance fenced per tenant, plans and limits per tenant, and a clean lifecycle for onboarding, export and deletion.

44
00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:12.781
