86
00:06:07.555 --> 00:06:08.429
Part 2.

87
00:06:08.709 --> 00:06:10.587
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

88
00:06:11.837 --> 00:06:13.991
What multi-tenancy is (and isn't).

89
00:06:14.271 --> 00:06:24.383
A tenant is an isolated customer of your system, usually a whole organization (a retail brand), not an individual user; a tenant has many users.

90
00:06:24.663 --> 00:06:34.263
Multi-tenancy means one running system serves many tenants who must not see or affect each other, while sharing infrastructure to keep unit costs low.

91
00:06:34.543 --> 00:06:40.687
It is the economic engine of SaaS: one codebase, one (or few) deployments, many customers.

92
00:06:42.037 --> 00:06:47.136
It is not the same as having many users, a single-tenant app can have millions of users.

93
00:06:47.416 --> 00:07:04.909
The defining requirement is isolation between tenants: data isolation (Acme never sees Globex's rows), performance isolation (Globex's burst doesn't slow Acme), and often configuration or branding isolation (each tenant's own settings, theme, plan).

94
00:07:05.189 --> 00:07:13.424
The central tension throughout: sharing lowers cost but weakens isolation; dedicating strengthens isolation but raises cost.

95
00:07:13.704 --> 00:07:16.648
Every decision in this chapter sits on that spectrum.

96
00:07:17.998 --> 00:07:20.963
The isolation spectrum: pool, bridge, silo.

97
00:07:21.243 --> 00:07:25.766
The industry (notably AWS's SaaS guidance) names three points:

98
00:07:26.046 --> 00:07:31.977
Silo, dedicated resources per tenant (own database, sometimes own app instance or stack).

99
00:07:32.257 --> 00:07:39.382
Strongest isolation, easiest per-tenant backup or restore or residency, simplest noisy neighbor story.

100
00:07:39.662 --> 00:07:43.886
Highest cost and operational overhead; hard at thousands of tenants.

101
00:07:44.166 --> 00:07:51.398
Pool, all tenants share the same resources (one database, one app fleet), separated logically by a tenantid.

102
00:07:51.678 --> 00:07:57.054
Cheapest and most scalable; isolation depends on software correctness (and RLS).

103
00:07:57.334 --> 00:08:00.299
Noisy-neighbor and blast-radius risks are highest.

104
00:08:01.649 --> 00:08:10.225
Bridge, a mix: shared app tier, but separated storage per tenant (schema-per-tenant), or pool for small tenants and silo for big ones.

105
00:08:10.505 --> 00:08:19.892
Most mature SaaS is not one model, it's pool for the long tail of small tenants and silo for large or regulated ones, chosen per tier.

106
00:08:20.172 --> 00:08:26.615
L1 builds all three storage variants on real PostgreSQL and shows the isolation each gives.

107
00:08:27.965 --> 00:08:30.205
Data isolation models in detail.

108
00:08:30.485 --> 00:08:31.829
Here is a quick table:

109
00:08:32.109 --> 00:08:39.682
Model, and Isolation, and Cost @ 10k tenants, and Per-tenant backup or restore, and Migrations, and Noisy neighbor.

110
00:08:39.962 --> 00:08:41.562
Shared table (tenantid).

111
00:08:41.842 --> 00:08:42.887
Isolation:

112
00:08:43.167 --> 00:08:44.959
Weakest (software-enforced).

113
00:08:45.239 --> 00:08:46.690
Cost @ 10k tenants:

114
00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:47.781
Lowest.

115
00:08:48.061 --> 00:08:49.725
Per-tenant backup or restore:

116
00:08:50.005 --> 00:08:51.626
Hard (filter by tenant).

117
00:08:51.906 --> 00:08:52.909
Migrations:

118
00:08:53.189 --> 00:08:55.365
One migration, all tenants at once.

119
00:08:55.645 --> 00:08:56.711
Noisy neighbor:

120
00:08:56.991 --> 00:08:57.738
Worst.

121
00:08:58.018 --> 00:08:59.874
Shared DB, schema-per-tenant.

122
00:09:00.154 --> 00:09:01.199
Isolation:

123
00:09:01.479 --> 00:09:02.290
Medium.

124
00:09:02.570 --> 00:09:04.021
Cost @ 10k tenants:

125
00:09:04.301 --> 00:09:05.111
Medium.

126
00:09:05.391 --> 00:09:07.055
Per-tenant backup or restore:

127
00:09:07.335 --> 00:09:08.786
Medium (per-schema).

128
00:09:09.066 --> 00:09:10.069
Migrations:

129
00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:12.610
Repeat per schema (10k times).

130
00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:13.957
Noisy neighbor:

131
00:09:14.237 --> 00:09:15.047
Medium.

132
00:09:15.327 --> 00:09:17.162
Database-per-tenant (silo).

133
00:09:17.442 --> 00:09:18.487
Isolation:

134
00:09:18.767 --> 00:09:19.685
Strongest.

135
00:09:19.965 --> 00:09:21.415
Cost @ 10k tenants:

136
00:09:21.695 --> 00:09:22.485
Highest.

137
00:09:22.765 --> 00:09:24.429
Per-tenant backup or restore:

138
00:09:24.709 --> 00:09:26.010
Easy (per-DB).

139
00:09:26.290 --> 00:09:27.293
Migrations:

140
00:09:27.573 --> 00:09:28.853
Repeat per DB.

141
00:09:29.133 --> 00:09:30.199
Noisy neighbor:

142
00:09:30.479 --> 00:09:31.226
Best.

143
00:09:32.576 --> 00:09:38.571
The shared-table model's fatal weakness is that isolation lives in every query (L1's leak).

144
00:09:38.851 --> 00:09:40.131
The two mitigations:

145
00:09:40.411 --> 00:09:51.483
Row-Level Security (L2) pushes the filter into the engine, and careful framework enforcement (a tenant-aware repository layer that refuses an unscoped query).

146
00:09:51.763 --> 00:10:03.027
Schema- and database-per-tenant move isolation into the topology, at the price of running migrations and connections N times (§2.10, Ch 28).

147
00:10:04.377 --> 00:10:07.022
Tenant context: resolve and propagate.

148
00:10:07.302 --> 00:10:11.910
Every request must be tagged with its tenant, and that tag must travel everywhere:

149
00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:29.747
Resolve the tenant at the edge from a trustworthy source: a subdomain (acme.shop.in), a path (/t or acme/...), or, best, a claim in the authenticated JWT (Ch 23), never from a user-supplied header or body a client could forge.

150
00:10:31.097 --> 00:10:44.452
Propagate it through the call stack: a request-scoped TenantContext (a ThreadLocal, or explicit parameter, or context propagated across async boundaries and to other services via a header or baggage).

151
00:10:44.732 --> 00:10:49.660
Losing tenant context across a thread pool or an async hop is a classic bug.

152
00:10:49.940 --> 00:11:04.340
Apply it at every tenant-aware resource: set app.tenant on the DB connection at checkout (L2), prefix cache keys, scope search queries, route to the tenant's database (silo), and stamp logs or metrics with the tenant.

153
00:11:05.690 --> 00:11:14.991
The golden rule: derive the tenant from the authenticated principal, once, at the boundary, and never trust a tenant id that came from the request body.

154
00:11:16.341 --> 00:11:18.688
Row-Level Security as the backstop.

155
00:11:18.968 --> 00:11:27.800
PostgreSQL Row-Level Security attaches a policy to a table so the engine automatically adds the tenant predicate to every statement.

156
00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:43.248
L2 shows a policy keyed to a per-connection setting (app.tenant): even SELECT FROM orders with no WHERE returns only the current tenant's rows, a cross-tenant UPDATE matches nothing, and with no tenant set it returns nothing (fails closed).

157
00:11:43.528 --> 00:11:46.707
Two non-negotiable cautions the lab demonstrates:

158
00:11:46.987 --> 00:11:50.037
Superusers and table owners bypass RLS.

159
00:11:50.317 --> 00:11:57.592
The app must connect as a limited, non-owner role (L2's first attempt leaked because it connected as the superuser).

160
00:11:57.872 --> 00:12:02.032
Use FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY if the owner must be subject to it.

161
00:12:03.382 --> 00:12:11.147
Set the tenant from the authenticated principal at connection checkout, never from client input, or you've just moved the injection point.

162
00:12:11.427 --> 00:12:20.899
RLS is defense in depth, not a license to stop filtering in the app, it's the backstop that makes a forgotten filter a non-event instead of a breach.

163
00:12:22.249 --> 00:12:24.980
Performance isolation and the noisy neighbor.

164
00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:34.177
In a pool model, tenants share CPU, connections, threads and I or O, so one tenant's spike can starve others, the noisy neighbor (L3).

165
00:12:34.457 --> 00:12:35.375
Defenses:

166
00:12:35.655 --> 00:12:43.463
Per-tenant concurrency caps: a tenant can hold at most K connections or N in-flight requests, so it can't drain the shared pool.

167
00:12:43.743 --> 00:12:58.676
Fair scheduling: per-tenant queues served round-robin or weighted fair queuing (weight by plan), so each tenant gets its share regardless of others' load (L3: quiet-tenant wait dropped from ~224 ticks to ~0).

168
00:12:58.956 --> 00:13:05.953
Per-tenant rate limits and quotas (L4): cap request rate and resource use per tenant or plan at admission.

169
00:13:07.303 --> 00:13:12.466
Bulkheads (Ch 18): separate pools so one tenant's saturation is contained.

170
00:13:12.746 --> 00:13:19.125
Silo the heavy tenants: a tenant big enough to be a permanent noisy neighbor gets dedicated resources.

171
00:13:19.405 --> 00:13:27.618
Performance isolation is about isolating throughput and latency, the complement to L1 or L2's isolation of data.

172
00:13:28.968 --> 00:13:31.443
Rate limiting, metering, and tiered plans.

173
00:13:31.723 --> 00:13:37.867
SaaS monetizes through plans (free or pro or enterprise) that differ in limits and features.

174
00:13:38.147 --> 00:13:39.299
Two mechanisms:

175
00:13:39.579 --> 00:13:48.581
Rate limiting at admission, a token bucket per tenant sized by plan (L4: free 5, pro 20, enterprise 200 capacity).

176
00:13:48.861 --> 00:14:00.104
It smooths bursts (the bucket allows a burst up to capacity, then throttles to the refill rate) and returns HTTP 429 with a Retry-After when empty.

177
00:14:00.384 --> 00:14:07.339
Implement it atomically and centrally (a Redis Lua script, L4) so limits hold across all app instances.

178
00:14:08.689 --> 00:14:22.257
Metering, count each tenant's billable usage (requests, orders, storage, API calls) into a per-tenant, per-period counter (L4's usage meter) that feeds usage-based billing and quota enforcement.

179
00:14:22.537 --> 00:14:25.971
Meter what you bill; make the meter accurate and idempotent.

180
00:14:26.251 --> 00:14:32.566
Feature gating (which features a plan unlocks) is usually done with per-tenant feature flags keyed on plan.

181
00:14:33.916 --> 00:14:37.009
Tenant lifecycle: onboarding, export, offboarding.

182
00:14:37.289 --> 00:14:50.089
Onboarding or provisioning: creating a tenant must be automated and fast, create the tenant record, (for silo) provision its schema or database and run migrations, seed defaults, set the plan.

183
00:14:50.369 --> 00:14:53.975
Manual onboarding doesn't scale past a handful of tenants.

184
00:14:54.255 --> 00:15:00.057
Export: tenants expect to get their data out (portability, and a legal right in many regimes).

185
00:15:00.337 --> 00:15:07.057
A per-tenant export is trivial in silo (dump the DB) and needs a careful tenant-scoped extract in pool.

186
00:15:08.407 --> 00:15:17.645
Offboarding or deletion: when a tenant leaves you must completely delete their data, every table, cache, search index, blob, backup-with-caveats.

187
00:15:17.925 --> 00:15:27.653
Silo makes this a DROP DATABASE; pool makes it a tenant-scoped delete across every store, which is exactly why knowing every place tenant data lives matters.

188
00:15:27.933 --> 00:15:34.354
This is both a trust and a compliance requirement (GDPR or DPDP right to erasure).

189
00:15:35.704 --> 00:15:39.267
Compliance: residency, encryption, and the tenant boundary.

190
00:15:39.547 --> 00:15:44.432
Data residency: some tenants (or laws) require data to stay in a region.

191
00:15:44.712 --> 00:15:51.368
Silo-by-region or a region-aware routing layer satisfies this; a single global pool usually can't.

192
00:15:51.648 --> 00:16:07.371
Per-tenant encryption: envelope encryption (Ch 23) with a per-tenant data key means a key can be revoked or rotated per tenant, and crypto-shredding (destroying a tenant's key) is a fast, provable deletion for that tenant's encrypted data.

193
00:16:08.721 --> 00:16:20.113
The tenant boundary is a security boundary: cross-tenant access is a top-severity vulnerability class (it's the SaaS form of IDOR or broken object-level authorization).

194
00:16:20.393 --> 00:16:29.481
Every authorization check must include the tenant (Ch 23); RLS (L2) is the data-layer enforcement of the same boundary.

195
00:16:30.831 --> 00:16:33.199
Scaling tenancy: sharding and cells.

196
00:16:33.479 --> 00:16:35.804
At high tenant counts the model must scale:

197
00:16:36.084 --> 00:16:44.873
Shard tenants across databases or clusters: partition by tenantid (Ch 11) so no single database holds all tenants.

198
00:16:45.153 --> 00:16:54.199
A tenant, then shard routing map (a lookup service) directs each request; you can place big tenants on their own shard and pack small ones together.

199
00:16:55.549 --> 00:17:01.757
Cell-based architecture: run many independent cells, each a full stack serving a subset of tenants.

200
00:17:02.037 --> 00:17:12.149
A cell is a blast-radius boundary, an incident, a bad deploy, or a noisy tenant is contained to one cell, and cells scale horizontally by adding more.

201
00:17:12.429 --> 00:17:18.658
This is how the largest SaaS platforms combine pool economics with silo-like isolation.

202
00:17:18.938 --> 00:17:36.026
The 10 or 1,000 or 100,000 tenants rule of thumb: database-per-tenant is fine at 10, painful at 1,000 (connection and migration overhead), impractical at 100,000 (pool + RLS + sharding + cells instead).

203
00:17:36.306 --> 00:17:38.525
Design for the count you'll actually have.

204
00:17:39.875 --> 00:17:41.304
Tenant-aware everything.

205
00:17:41.584 --> 00:17:45.808
Multi-tenancy touches every subsystem, and forgetting one is a leak:

206
00:17:46.088 --> 00:17:55.837
Cache (Ch 07): prefix every key with the tenant (t:acme:cart:42) or a cache-per-tenant, or one tenant's data serves another's request.

207
00:17:56.117 --> 00:18:01.877
Search (Ch 22): a per-tenant index or a mandatory tenant filter on every query.

208
00:18:02.157 --> 00:18:10.925
Messaging (Ch 14): tenant on every message; consumers scope by it; consider per-tenant topics or partitions for isolation.

209
00:18:12.275 --> 00:18:23.646
Observability (Ch 24): stamp logs, metrics and traces with the tenant so you can see per-tenant health, cost and SLOs, and debug one tenant without wading through all.

210
00:18:23.926 --> 00:18:29.153
