Design Document (excerpt), Multi-Tenancy Standard v1.0.

243
00:20:19.309 --> 00:20:20.205
Status:

244
00:20:20.485 --> 00:20:21.765
Accepted, Authors:

245
00:20:22.045 --> 00:20:24.243
Platform Architecture Guild, Reviewers:

246
00:20:24.523 --> 00:20:26.400
Security, SRE, Billing.

247
00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:30.179
Context, The platform is SaaS for many retail brands.

248
00:20:30.459 --> 00:20:39.952
A cross-tenant data leak (a report missing its tenant filter) and recurring noisy-neighbor slowdowns showed tenancy was not enforced, only convention.

249
00:20:40.232 --> 00:20:43.347
Decision, Tenancy is a first-class platform property.

250
00:20:43.627 --> 00:21:00.459
Isolation is enforced by the database (RLS), not by query discipline; big or regulated tenants are siloed; performance is fenced per tenant; plans set per-tenant limits; a standard lifecycle governs onboarding or export or deletion.

251
00:21:01.809 --> 00:21:18.705
Related ADRs, ADR-160 Pool+RLS for standard tenants, silo (dedicated DB) for enterprise or regulated; ADR-161 Tenant from JWT claim only, propagated via TenantContext,

252
00:21:18.985 --> 00:21:50.103
set as app.tenant at DB checkout; ADR-162 App connects as a non-owner role so RLS applies; ADR-163 Per-tenant rate limits + concurrency caps + fair queuing; ADR-164 Usage metering per tenant for billing; ADR-165 Automated provisioning + tenant export + hard-delete (crypto-shred); ADR-166 Cell-based topology beyond N tenants.

253
00:21:50.383 --> 00:21:51.343
Requirements.

254
00:21:52.693 --> 00:21:55.317
R1, No cross-tenant read or write, ever.

255
00:21:55.597 --> 00:22:00.589
Enforced at the data layer (RLS or silo), not only in application code.

256
00:22:00.869 --> 00:22:02.789
A forgotten filter MUST NOT leak.

257
00:22:03.069 --> 00:22:04.349
(L1, L2.).

258
00:22:04.629 --> 00:22:06.485
R2, Tenant from identity.

259
00:22:06.765 --> 00:22:14.872
The tenant is derived from the authenticated principal at the edge and never accepted from request bodies or headers a client controls.

260
00:22:15.152 --> 00:22:16.603
(§2.4.).

261
00:22:16.883 --> 00:22:18.931
R3, Performance isolation.

262
00:22:19.211 --> 00:22:27.360
No tenant may consume more than its per-plan share of connections or CPU; noisy tenants MUST NOT breach other tenants' SLOs.

263
00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:29.027
(L3, L4.).

264
00:22:29.307 --> 00:22:31.632
R4, Metered and limited by plan.

265
00:22:31.912 --> 00:22:38.525
Every tenant has a plan; limits are enforced centrally and atomically; billable usage is metered per tenant.

266
00:22:38.805 --> 00:22:39.701
(L4.).

267
00:22:41.051 --> 00:22:42.886
R5, Clean lifecycle.

268
00:22:43.166 --> 00:22:48.009
Onboarding is automated; export and complete deletion are supported and audited.

269
00:22:48.289 --> 00:22:49.697
(§2.8.).

270
00:22:49.977 --> 00:22:51.982
R6, Residency and keys.

271
00:22:52.262 --> 00:22:58.598
Regulated tenants can be pinned to a region and have a per-tenant encryption key (crypto-shred on deletion).

272
00:22:58.878 --> 00:23:00.307
(§2.9.).

273
00:23:00.587 --> 00:23:01.995
Decisions in detail.

274
00:23:02.275 --> 00:23:08.163
Pool + RLS default, silo for big or regulated (ADR-160).

275
00:23:08.443 --> 00:23:18.065
Standard tenants share DBs with RLS; enterprise or regulated tenants get a dedicated database (residency, backup, blast radius).

276
00:23:18.345 --> 00:23:19.625
(L1, L2.).

277
00:23:20.975 --> 00:23:25.433
Tenant context (ADR-161 or 162).

278
00:23:25.713 --> 00:23:34.289
JWT claim, then TenantContext, then SET app.tenant at checkout, app connects as a limited role so RLS binds.

279
00:23:34.569 --> 00:23:35.423
(L2.).

280
00:23:35.703 --> 00:23:38.860
Performance fences (ADR-163).

281
00:23:39.140 --> 00:23:43.513
Per-tenant connection caps + weighted fair queuing + per-tenant rate limits.

282
00:23:43.793 --> 00:23:45.180
(L3, L4.).

283
00:23:45.460 --> 00:23:47.977
Metering (ADR-164).

284
00:23:48.257 --> 00:23:51.095
Atomic per-tenant counters feed billing and quotas.

285
00:23:51.375 --> 00:23:52.271
(L4.).

286
00:23:52.551 --> 00:23:55.367
Lifecycle (ADR-165).

287
00:23:55.647 --> 00:24:01.172
Automated provision; export; hard-delete across every store; crypto-shred the tenant key.

288
00:24:02.522 --> 00:24:04.954
Cells (ADR-166).

289
00:24:05.234 --> 00:24:09.863
Beyond a set tenant count, run independent cells as blast-radius boundaries.

290
00:24:10.143 --> 00:24:11.850
(§2.10.).

291
00:24:12.130 --> 00:24:13.154
Consequences.

292
00:24:13.434 --> 00:24:22.821
More platform machinery (RLS, tenant context plumbing, fair scheduling, metering, provisioning automation, cells) and a per-tenant tier decision.

293
00:24:23.101 --> 00:24:30.887
In return: no cross-tenant leaks, contained blast radius, fair performance, monetizable plans, and compliant lifecycle.

294
00:24:31.167 --> 00:24:34.581
The just add a tenantid column era is explicitly ended.

