00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.458
THE NOISY-NEIGHBOR PROBLEM and its fix.

368
00:32:05.738 --> 00:32:10.581
Three tenants share ONE worker (a thread pool or DB connection or CPU).

369
00:32:10.861 --> 00:32:19.181
The. noisy tenant dumps a 300-task burst at t=0; two quiet tenants submit a few tasks each, spread over time.

370
00:32:19.461 --> 00:32:20.335
We run the.

371
00:32:23.185 --> 00:32:24.103
On screen:

372
00:32:24.383 --> 00:32:25.449
Noisy neighbor:

373
00:32:25.729 --> 00:32:32.449
'noisy' bursts 300 tasks at once; 'quietA'/'quietB' submit 20 tasks. each, spread out.

374
00:32:32.729 --> 00:32:33.924
One shared worker.

375
00:32:34.204 --> 00:32:37.703
Wait time = ticks a task waited before being served.

376
00:32:37.983 --> 00:33:02.985
FIFO (one shared queue, arrival order): noisy tasks=300 mean wait= 149.5 ticks max wait= 299 ticks. quietA tasks=20 mean wait= 224.0 ticks max wait= 300 ticks. quietB tasks=20 mean wait= 222.0 ticks max wait= 298 ticks.

377
00:33:03.265 --> 00:33:26.284
FAIR (per-tenant queues, round-robin): noisy tasks=300 mean wait= 179.2 ticks max wait= 339 ticks. quietA tasks=20 mean wait= 0.1 ticks max wait= 1 ticks. quietB tasks=20 mean wait= 0.0 ticks max wait= 0 ticks.

378
00:33:26.564 --> 00:33:28.079
The rest is in the chapter.

379
00:33:30.929 --> 00:33:31.782
Reading it:

380
00:33:32.062 --> 00:33:43.582
FIFO: the burst lands first, so the quiet tenants, who barely use the system, wait a mean of ~224 / ~222 ticks purely because of a neighbor.

381
00:33:43.862 --> 00:33:46.849
Their latency is hostage to someone else's load.

382
00:33:47.129 --> 00:34:04.878
Fair queuing: per-tenant queues served round-robin drop the quiet tenants' wait to ~0.1 or 0.0 ticks; the noisy tenant's mean rises slightly (149, then 179) because it now yields turns instead of monopolizing the worker.

383
00:34:05.158 --> 00:34:08.678
It still gets its full share, it just can't steal everyone else's.

384
00:34:10.028 --> 00:34:15.319
This is throughput isolation, the complement to L1 or L2's data isolation.

385
00:34:15.599 --> 00:34:26.308
In production the same idea appears as per-tenant connection-pool caps, per-tenant thread limits, weighted fair queuing (weight by plan), and admission-level rate limits (L4).

