After 4 s idle, the free bucket allowed 2 of 10, it refilled ~0.5 token per second times 4 s about 2 tokens.

415
00:37:30.300 --> 00:37:37.575
A token bucket is steady-state, not a fixed-window reset: capacity sets the burst, refill sets the sustained rate.

416
00:37:37.855 --> 00:37:48.244
Doing this in Redis with Lua makes it atomic and global, every app instance shares one authoritative bucket, so concurrent requests across the fleet can't race past the limit.

417
00:37:48.524 --> 00:37:52.961
Return 429 with Retry-After when the bucket is empty.

418
00:37:54.311 --> 00:37:55.250
Part 6.

419
00:37:55.530 --> 00:37:56.810
Production Concerns.

420
00:37:58.060 --> 00:37:59.212
Failure modes.

421
00:37:59.492 --> 00:38:00.836
Here is a quick table:

422
00:38:01.116 --> 00:38:03.313
Failure, and Cause, and Mitigation.

423
00:38:03.593 --> 00:38:05.001
Cross-tenant data leak.

424
00:38:05.281 --> 00:38:06.049
Cause:

425
00:38:06.329 --> 00:38:09.039
Forgotten WHERE tenantid in shared-table model.

426
00:38:09.319 --> 00:38:10.321
Mitigation:

427
00:38:10.601 --> 00:38:16.255
RLS backstop (L2); tenant-aware repository; silo for sensitive tenants.

428
00:38:16.535 --> 00:38:18.412
RLS silently not applied.

429
00:38:18.692 --> 00:38:19.460
Cause:

430
00:38:19.740 --> 00:38:23.452
App connects as owner or superuser (bypasses RLS).

431
00:38:23.732 --> 00:38:24.735
Mitigation:

432
00:38:25.015 --> 00:38:29.580
Connect as a limited non-owner role; FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY (L2).

433
00:38:29.860 --> 00:38:31.311
Tenant context lost.

434
00:38:31.591 --> 00:38:32.359
Cause:

435
00:38:32.639 --> 00:38:36.393
Not propagated across async or thread-pool or service hops.

436
00:38:36.673 --> 00:38:37.676
Mitigation:

437
00:38:37.956 --> 00:38:42.756
Explicit context propagation; assert tenant is set before any DB call.

438
00:38:43.036 --> 00:38:44.508
Tenant from user input.

439
00:38:44.788 --> 00:38:45.556
Cause:

440
00:38:45.836 --> 00:38:48.055
Trusting a body or header tenant id.

441
00:38:48.335 --> 00:38:49.337
Mitigation:

442
00:38:49.617 --> 00:38:54.353
Derive from the authenticated JWT only (§2.4).

443
00:38:54.633 --> 00:38:55.700
Noisy neighbor.

444
00:38:55.980 --> 00:38:56.748
Cause:

445
00:38:57.028 --> 00:38:59.033
Shared pool, no per-tenant limits.

446
00:38:59.313 --> 00:39:00.316
Mitigation:

447
00:39:00.596 --> 00:39:05.481
Concurrency caps, fair queuing, rate limits (L3, L4), silo the whale.

448
00:39:06.831 --> 00:39:08.154
Incomplete deletion.

449
00:39:08.434 --> 00:39:09.202
Cause:

450
00:39:09.482 --> 00:39:11.701
Tenant data spread across many stores.

451
00:39:11.981 --> 00:39:12.983
Mitigation:

452
00:39:13.263 --> 00:39:20.111
Inventory every store; automated tenant-scoped delete; crypto-shred (§2.8 or 2.9).

453
00:39:20.391 --> 00:39:22.354
One tenant's bad deploy hits all.

454
00:39:22.634 --> 00:39:23.402
Cause:

455
00:39:23.682 --> 00:39:25.538
Single global blast radius.

456
00:39:25.818 --> 00:39:26.821
Mitigation:

457
00:39:27.101 --> 00:39:30.258
Cell-based architecture (§2.10).

458
00:39:30.538 --> 00:39:32.159
Runaway cost on free tier.

459
00:39:32.439 --> 00:39:33.207
Cause:

460
00:39:33.487 --> 00:39:35.493
No per-tenant metering or limits.

461
00:39:35.773 --> 00:39:36.775
Mitigation:

462
00:39:37.055 --> 00:39:39.999
Metering + quotas + plan limits (L4).

463
00:39:41.349 --> 00:39:43.568
Observability (Ch 24).

464
00:39:43.848 --> 00:39:51.251
Stamp everything with the tenant, logs, metrics, traces, so you have per-tenant SLOs, error rates, latency, and cost.

465
00:39:51.531 --> 00:39:56.587
A dashboard by tenant is how you spot a noisy neighbor or a single tenant's outage.

466
00:39:56.867 --> 00:40:05.059
Alert on cross-tenant access attempts (an RLS deny, an authz failure with a tenant mismatch), these are security signals.

467
00:40:05.339 --> 00:40:12.208
Track per-tenant usage versus plan limits to spot tenants about to hit quota (upsell) or abusing the free tier.

468
00:40:13.558 --> 00:40:15.563
Security (Ch 23).

469
00:40:15.843 --> 00:40:27.363
The tenant is part of every authorization decision, object-level checks must verify the object belongs to the caller's tenant (cross-tenant IDOR is the 1 SaaS vuln class).

470
00:40:27.643 --> 00:40:34.299
RLS is the data-layer enforcement of the tenant boundary (L2); connect as a least-privilege role.

471
00:40:34.579 --> 00:40:41.918
Per-tenant encryption keys enable revocation and crypto-shredding; store tenant keys in a KMS, never with the data.

472
00:40:42.198 --> 00:40:51.649
Test cross-tenant isolation explicitly (Ch 29): a test that tenant A cannot read or modify tenant B for every resource.

473
00:40:52.999 --> 00:40:54.364
Performance and tuning.

474
00:40:54.644 --> 00:40:59.167
Per-tenant connection caps stop one tenant draining the pool; size them by plan.

475
00:40:59.447 --> 00:41:06.679
Token-bucket capacity versus refill: capacity sets the allowed burst, refill sets the sustained rate (L4).

476
00:41:06.959 --> 00:41:16.537
In silo, connection count multiplies by tenants, use a proxy or pooler (PgBouncer) or you'll exhaust maxconnections at a few hundred tenants.

477
00:41:16.817 --> 00:41:28.209
RLS adds the tenant predicate to every query, ensure tenantid is the leading column of the relevant indexes (composite (tenantid,...)), or every query does extra work.

478
00:41:29.559 --> 00:41:30.285
Cost.

479
00:41:30.565 --> 00:41:43.066
Cost per tenant is a first-class metric: attribute compute, storage and I or O to tenants (via the tenant stamp) so finance knows margins per plan and which tenants are unprofitable.

480
00:41:43.346 --> 00:41:49.063
Pool is cheapest per tenant but risks noisy neighbors; silo is priciest but predictable.

481
00:41:49.343 --> 00:41:57.343
Place tenants on the model whose cost matches their revenue, free-tier tenants pooled densely, enterprise tenants siloed and paying for it.

482
00:41:58.693 --> 00:41:59.632
Part 7.

483
00:41:59.912 --> 00:42:01.619
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

484
00:42:02.869 --> 00:42:03.786
Watch out:

485
00:42:04.066 --> 00:42:04.706
1.

486
00:42:04.986 --> 00:42:07.397
Isolation by query discipline alone.

487
00:42:07.677 --> 00:42:15.421
Relying on every query to remember WHERE tenantid=? guarantees a leak eventually (§1.1, L1).

488
00:42:15.701 --> 00:42:18.901
Add an engine-level backstop (RLS) or silo.

489
00:42:20.251 --> 00:42:21.168
Watch out:

490
00:42:21.448 --> 00:42:22.152
2.

491
00:42:22.432 --> 00:42:25.525
RLS while connecting as a superuser or owner.

492
00:42:25.805 --> 00:42:32.184
Superusers and table owners bypass RLS, your policy does nothing (L2's first run leaked).

493
00:42:32.464 --> 00:42:35.707
Connect as a limited non-owner role; FORCE for the owner.

494
00:42:37.057 --> 00:42:37.974
Watch out:

495
00:42:38.254 --> 00:42:39.001
3.

496
00:42:39.281 --> 00:42:41.457
Trusting a tenant id from the request.

497
00:42:41.737 --> 00:42:46.003
A tenantid in the body or a header can be forged to access another tenant.

498
00:42:46.283 --> 00:42:51.681
Derive the tenant from the authenticated JWT only (§2.4).

499
00:42:53.031 --> 00:42:53.948
Watch out:

500
00:42:54.228 --> 00:42:54.932
4.

501
00:42:55.212 --> 00:42:58.007
Losing tenant context across async boundaries.

502
00:42:58.287 --> 00:43:05.817
A ThreadLocal tenant that isn't propagated into a thread pool or async task silently becomes null or another tenant's.

503
00:43:06.097 --> 00:43:09.745
Propagate explicitly; assert it's set before DB access.

504
00:43:11.095 --> 00:43:12.013
Watch out:

505
00:43:12.293 --> 00:43:13.061
5.

506
00:43:13.341 --> 00:43:14.962
No performance isolation.

507
00:43:15.242 --> 00:43:22.495
A shared pool with no per-tenant limits lets one tenant's batch job starve everyone (§1.1, L3).

508
00:43:22.775 --> 00:43:25.186
Cap concurrency, fair-queue, rate-limit.

509
00:43:26.536 --> 00:43:27.453
Watch out:

510
00:43:27.733 --> 00:43:28.480
6.

511
00:43:28.760 --> 00:43:30.211
One plan for everyone.

512
00:43:30.491 --> 00:43:34.843
No per-tenant limits means a free or abusive tenant degrades paying ones.

513
00:43:35.123 --> 00:43:38.259
Tiered plans + rate limits + metering (L4).

514
00:43:39.609 --> 00:43:40.526
Watch out:

515
00:43:40.806 --> 00:43:41.553
7.

516
00:43:41.833 --> 00:43:45.865
Un-prefixed cache keys or shared search index with no tenant filter.

517
00:43:46.145 --> 00:43:50.987
A cache key or search query without the tenant serves one tenant's data to another.

518
00:43:51.267 --> 00:43:55.427
Prefix keys; scope every index query (§2.11).

519
00:43:56.777 --> 00:43:57.695
Watch out:

520
00:43:57.975 --> 00:43:58.657
8.

521
00:43:58.937 --> 00:44:01.391
Database-per-tenant at 10,000 tenants.

522
00:44:01.671 --> 00:44:05.084
Connections, migrations and operational load explode.

523
00:44:05.364 --> 00:44:11.337
Pool + RLS + sharding + cells at high tenant counts (§2.10).

524
00:44:12.687 --> 00:44:13.605
Watch out:

525
00:44:13.885 --> 00:44:14.631
9.

526
00:44:14.911 --> 00:44:16.874
Manual onboarding or offboarding.

527
00:44:17.154 --> 00:44:24.002
Hand-written provisioning and deletion scripts don't scale and miss stores (incomplete deletion is a compliance breach).

528
00:44:24.282 --> 00:44:26.970
Automate the lifecycle (§2.8).

529
00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:29.237
Watch out:

530
00:44:29.517 --> 00:44:30.221
10.

531
00:44:30.501 --> 00:44:32.208
Tenant-blind observability.

532
00:44:32.488 --> 00:44:38.120
Metrics aggregated across all tenants hide a single tenant's outage or noisy behavior.

533
00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:43.008
Stamp everything with the tenant (§2.11, Ch 24).

534
00:44:44.358 --> 00:44:45.275
Watch out:

535
00:44:45.555 --> 00:44:46.387
11.

536
00:44:46.667 --> 00:44:48.886
Forgetting the tenant in authorization.

537
00:44:49.166 --> 00:44:56.078
Object-level checks that don't verify tenant ownership are cross-tenant IDOR, a top SaaS vulnerability.

538
00:44:56.358 --> 00:44:59.942
Tenant is part of every authz decision (Ch 23).

539
00:45:01.292 --> 00:45:02.209
Watch out:

540
00:45:02.489 --> 00:45:03.279
12.

541
00:45:03.559 --> 00:45:05.393
No per-tenant cost attribution.

542
00:45:05.673 --> 00:45:10.879
Without it you can't tell which tenants or plans are profitable and you'll subsidize abusers.

543
00:45:11.159 --> 00:45:14.295
Attribute cost per tenant (§6.5).

544
00:45:15.645 --> 00:45:16.519
Part 8.

545
00:45:16.799 --> 00:45:18.143
Interview Q and A.

546
00:45:19.393 --> 00:45:20.225
Beginner.

547
00:45:20.505 --> 00:45:21.529
Question 1.

548
00:45:21.809 --> 00:45:25.244
What is multi-tenancy and why do SaaS products use it?

549
00:45:25.524 --> 00:45:26.889
Thirty-second answer:

550
00:45:27.169 --> 00:45:39.671
Multi-tenancy means one running system serves many isolated customers (tenants, usually whole organizations), sharing infrastructure while keeping each tenant's data and performance separate.

551
00:45:39.951 --> 00:45:53.199
SaaS uses it because sharing one codebase and few deployments across many customers is what makes the unit economics work; the hard part is enforcing isolation so tenants never see or affect each other.

552
00:45:54.549 --> 00:45:55.551
Question 2.

553
00:45:55.831 --> 00:45:58.370
Describe the three data isolation models.

554
00:45:58.650 --> 00:46:00.015
Thirty-second answer:

555
00:46:00.295 --> 00:46:07.698
(1) Shared table, one table with a tenantid column; cheapest, weakest isolation (a forgotten filter leaks).

556
00:46:07.978 --> 00:46:13.973
(2) Schema-per-tenant, one schema per tenant in a shared database; medium isolation and cost.

557
00:46:14.253 --> 00:46:23.597
(3) Database-per-tenant (silo), a separate database per tenant; strongest isolation, easy per-tenant backup or residency, highest cost.

558
00:46:23.877 --> 00:46:27.119
Most SaaS pools small tenants and siloes big ones.

559
00:46:28.469 --> 00:46:29.515
Question 3.

560
00:46:29.795 --> 00:46:32.483
How does a request know which tenant it belongs to?

561
00:46:32.763 --> 00:46:34.128
Thirty-second answer:

562
00:46:34.408 --> 00:46:46.099
You resolve the tenant at the edge from a trustworthy source, ideally a claim in the authenticated JWT, or a subdomain or path, never from a header or body the client controls.

563
00:46:46.379 --> 00:46:54.699
Then you propagate it through the whole request (a tenant context) and apply it everywhere: the DB, the cache, the search index, logs.

564
00:46:56.049 --> 00:46:57.115
Question 4.

565
00:46:57.395 --> 00:46:59.230
What is the 'noisy neighbor' problem?

566
00:46:59.510 --> 00:47:00.875
Thirty-second answer:

567
00:47:01.155 --> 00:47:15.513
In a shared (pool) model, tenants share CPU, connections and I or O, so one tenant's spike, a big batch job, a traffic burst, can consume the shared resources and slow down or break other tenants who did nothing wrong.

568
00:47:15.793 --> 00:47:17.841
It's a performance-isolation failure.

569
00:47:19.191 --> 00:47:20.193
Intermediate.

570
00:47:20.473 --> 00:47:21.583
Question 5.

571
00:47:21.863 --> 00:47:25.617
What is Row-Level Security and how does it help multi-tenancy?

572
00:47:25.897 --> 00:47:27.263
Thirty-second answer:

573
00:47:27.543 --> 00:47:38.849
RLS is a database feature that attaches a policy to a table so the engine automatically adds a predicate to every query, for multi-tenancy, tenantid = currenttenant.

574
00:47:39.129 --> 00:47:50.372
Then even a query that forgets the tenant filter returns only the current tenant's rows, a cross-tenant update matches nothing, and with no tenant set it returns nothing (fails closed).

575
00:47:50.652 --> 00:47:54.940
It's the backstop that makes a forgotten filter a non-event instead of a breach.

576
00:47:56.290 --> 00:47:57.399
Question 6.

577
00:47:57.679 --> 00:48:00.901
How do you implement per-tenant rate limiting and tiered plans?

578
00:48:01.181 --> 00:48:02.546
Thirty-second answer:

579
00:48:02.826 --> 00:48:09.930
A token bucket per tenant, sized by plan (capacity = allowed burst, refill rate = sustained rate).

580
00:48:10.210 --> 00:48:21.389
Enforce it centrally and atomically, a Redis Lua script, so the limit holds across all app instances; return 429 with Retry-After when the bucket is empty.

581
00:48:21.669 --> 00:48:28.645
Separately meter each tenant's billable usage into a per-period counter for usage-based billing and quota checks.

582
00:48:29.995 --> 00:48:31.083
Question 7.

583
00:48:31.363 --> 00:48:34.712
Shared-table versus database-per-tenant, how do you choose?

584
00:48:34.992 --> 00:48:36.357
Thirty-second answer:

585
00:48:36.637 --> 00:48:37.704
By tenant tier.

586
00:48:37.984 --> 00:48:44.469
Shared-table (pool) for the long tail of small tenants, cheapest, most scalable, isolate with RLS.

587
00:48:44.749 --> 00:48:57.741
Database-per-tenant (silo) for large or regulated tenants, strongest isolation, easy per-tenant backup and residency, contained blast radius, but expensive and operationally heavy at scale.

588
00:48:58.021 --> 00:49:00.453
Many SaaS run both simultaneously.

589
00:49:01.803 --> 00:49:02.827
Question 8.

590
00:49:03.107 --> 00:49:05.603
How do you make sure tenant deletion is complete?

591
00:49:05.883 --> 00:49:07.249
Thirty-second answer:

592
00:49:07.529 --> 00:49:19.625
Know every place a tenant's data lives, every table, cache, search index, blob store, and message log, and delete from all of them, ideally through an automated, audited offboarding job.

593
00:49:19.905 --> 00:49:24.918
In silo it's a DROP DATABASE; in pool it's a tenant-scoped delete across each store.

594
00:49:25.198 --> 00:49:31.790
Per-tenant encryption enables crypto-shredding, destroy the tenant's key and the encrypted data is irrecoverable.

595
00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:34.207
Question 9.

596
00:49:34.487 --> 00:49:38.647
Why prefix cache keys with the tenant, and what else needs tenant-awareness?

597
00:49:38.927 --> 00:49:40.292
Thirty-second answer:

598
00:49:40.572 --> 00:49:49.617
Because a shared cache keyed only by, say, cart:42 would serve tenant A's cart for tenant B's request, a cross-tenant leak through the cache.

599
00:49:49.897 --> 00:49:53.161
Prefix keys with the tenant (t:acme:cart:42).

600
00:49:53.441 --> 00:50:05.665
The same applies to search indexes (per-tenant index or mandatory filter), messaging (tenant on every message), logs or metrics (stamp with tenant), and config or feature flags.

601
00:50:07.015 --> 00:50:07.783
Senior.

602
00:50:08.063 --> 00:50:09.109
Question 10.

603
00:50:09.389 --> 00:50:15.319
Design the data layer for a SaaS with 50,000 small tenants and 50 enterprise tenants.

604
00:50:15.599 --> 00:50:16.965
Thirty-second answer:

605
00:50:17.245 --> 00:50:18.162
Two tiers.

606
00:50:18.442 --> 00:50:30.922
The 50,000 small tenants go pool + RLS, sharded across several databases by tenantid (no single DB holds all), with a tenant, then shard routing map and a connection pooler.

607
00:50:31.202 --> 00:50:39.138
The 50 enterprise tenants get silo, a dedicated database each (residency, backup, blast radius, custom SLAs).

608
00:50:39.418 --> 00:50:41.978
Both share the app tier, with per-tenant limits.

609
00:50:42.258 --> 00:50:45.693
Consider cells to cap blast radius for the pooled tenants.

610
00:50:47.043 --> 00:50:48.173
Question 11.

611
00:50:48.453 --> 00:50:51.184
How do you prevent cross-tenant access at every layer?

612
00:50:51.464 --> 00:50:52.829
Thirty-second answer:

613
00:50:53.109 --> 00:50:54.261
Defense in depth:

614
00:50:54.541 --> 00:51:17.731
(1) derive tenant from the authenticated JWT at the edge, never from input; (2) include the tenant in every authorization check (object-level ownership); (3) enforce it at the data layer with RLS (pool) or physical separation (silo); (4) prefix caches, scope search, stamp messages; (5) test isolation explicitly for every resource.

615
00:51:18.011 --> 00:51:19.995
No single layer is trusted alone.

616
00:51:21.345 --> 00:51:22.433
Question 12.

617
00:51:22.713 --> 00:51:25.913
How do you achieve performance isolation in a pool model?

618
00:51:26.193 --> 00:51:27.558
Thirty-second answer:

619
00:51:27.838 --> 00:51:42.579
Per-tenant concurrency caps (a tenant can hold at most K connections or N in-flight requests), fair scheduling (per-tenant queues served round-robin or weighted by plan), per-tenant rate limits (L4), and bulkheads (separate pools).

620
00:51:42.859 --> 00:51:45.867
A tenant big enough to be a permanent problem gets siloed.

621
00:51:46.147 --> 00:51:49.539
The goal: one tenant's load can't breach another's SLO.

622
00:51:50.889 --> 00:51:52.169
Question 13.

623
00:51:52.449 --> 00:51:56.161
How does RLS interact with connection pooling, and what breaks?

624
00:51:56.441 --> 00:51:57.807
Thirty-second answer:

625
00:51:58.087 --> 00:52:06.684
RLS reads the tenant from a per-connection setting (app.tenant), but a connection pool reuses connections across requests or tenants.

626
00:52:06.964 --> 00:52:14.857
If you set app.tenant and don't reset it, the next request on that pooled connection inherits the previous tenant's context, a leak.

627
00:52:15.137 --> 00:52:25.505
So you must set the tenant at checkout and reset it at return (or use SET LOCAL inside the request's transaction so it's scoped and auto-cleared on commit or rollback).

628
00:52:26.855 --> 00:52:28.050
Question 14.

629
00:52:28.330 --> 00:52:32.639
How do you migrate schemas across thousands of tenants (Ch 28)?

630
00:52:32.919 --> 00:52:34.285
Thirty-second answer:

631
00:52:34.565 --> 00:52:41.434
For pool (shared table), one expand or contract migration covers all tenants at once, that's a big advantage.

632
00:52:41.714 --> 00:52:57.693
For silo or schema-per-tenant, you must run the migration against every database or schema, which you do as an orchestrated, resumable rollout (batch the tenants, track per-tenant migration state, retry failures) rather than one giant transaction.

633
00:52:57.973 --> 00:53:05.525
Either way the changes are backward-compatible (expand or contract) so old and new code coexist during the rollout.

634
00:53:06.875 --> 00:53:07.771
Architect.

635
00:53:08.051 --> 00:53:09.245
Question 15.

636
00:53:09.525 --> 00:53:12.427
What is cell-based architecture and when do you need it?

637
00:53:12.707 --> 00:53:14.072
Thirty-second answer:

638
00:53:14.352 --> 00:53:20.325
You run many independent cells, each a complete stack (app + data) serving a subset of tenants.

639
00:53:20.605 --> 00:53:29.885
A cell is a blast-radius boundary: a bad deploy, an incident, or a noisy tenant is contained to one cell instead of taking down all tenants.

640
00:53:30.165 --> 00:53:35.285
You scale by adding cells and route each tenant to its cell via a thin routing layer.

641
00:53:35.565 --> 00:53:41.219
It's how the largest SaaS platforms get pool economics with silo-like fault isolation.

642
00:53:42.569 --> 00:53:43.763
Question 16.

643
00:53:44.043 --> 00:53:47.265
How do you handle data residency for regulated tenants?

644
00:53:47.545 --> 00:53:48.910
Thirty-second answer:

645
00:53:49.190 --> 00:54:01.009
Pin those tenants to a region: silo-by-region (their database and processing live in the required region) with a region-aware routing layer that sends their requests only to that region.

646
00:54:01.289 --> 00:54:10.334
A single global pool usually can't satisfy residency, so residency-bound tenants typically get dedicated, region-local infrastructure.

647
00:54:10.614 --> 00:54:14.155
Combine with per-tenant encryption keys managed in-region.

648
00:54:15.505 --> 00:54:16.849
Question 17.

649
00:54:17.129 --> 00:54:21.161
How do you meter usage accurately for billing across a distributed system?

650
00:54:21.441 --> 00:54:22.807
Thirty-second answer:

651
00:54:23.087 --> 00:54:35.737
Emit a usage event per billable action, tagged with tenant, and aggregate into per-tenant, per-period counters, idempotently, so retries don't double-count (dedup by an event id, Ch 20).

652
00:54:36.017 --> 00:54:39.388
Store raw events (for dispute or audit) and roll them up.

653
00:54:39.668 --> 00:54:43.188
Reconcile the meter against the source of truth periodically.

654
00:54:43.468 --> 00:54:46.348
Enforce quotas off the same counters (L4).

655
00:54:47.698 --> 00:54:48.871
Question 18.

656
00:54:49.151 --> 00:54:52.522
A single tenant is 100 times larger than any other.

657
00:54:52.802 --> 00:54:53.975
How do you handle it?

658
00:54:54.255 --> 00:54:55.621
Thirty-second answer:

659
00:54:55.901 --> 00:54:56.797
Silo it.

660
00:54:57.077 --> 00:55:11.114
A tenant that large is a permanent noisy neighbor and a blast-radius risk in any pool, give it dedicated resources (its own database, possibly its own cell), tuned and scaled for its load, on a plan that pays for it.

661
00:55:11.394 --> 00:55:13.655
Keep the thousands of small tenants pooled.

662
00:55:13.935 --> 00:55:16.965
This pool the small, silo the whale split is standard.

663
00:55:18.315 --> 00:55:19.552
Question 19.

664
00:55:19.832 --> 00:55:23.459
How do you know and control per-tenant cost and profitability?

665
00:55:23.739 --> 00:55:25.104
Thirty-second answer:

666
00:55:25.384 --> 00:55:40.488
Attribute resource use to tenants: stamp compute or storage or I or O and API calls with the tenant (the same tenant tag used in observability), roll it into a per-tenant cost figure, and compare to per-tenant revenue.

667
00:55:40.768 --> 00:55:52.160
Then act: pool dense free tenants cheaply, ensure enterprise silos are priced above their cost, rate-limit or meter abusers, and flag unprofitable tenants for plan changes.

668
00:55:53.510 --> 00:55:54.619
Question 20.

669
00:55:54.899 --> 00:55:57.907
You're converting a single-tenant app to multi-tenant.

670
00:55:58.187 --> 00:55:59.169
What's your plan?

671
00:55:59.449 --> 00:56:00.814
Thirty-second answer:

672
00:56:01.094 --> 00:56:18.331
(1) Add tenantid everywhere and a tenant model; (2) resolve or propagate tenant context from auth; (3) enforce isolation at the data layer (RLS now, silo for those who need it), not by query discipline; (4) add per-tenant limits, metering,

673
00:56:18.611 --> 00:56:35.849
and plans; (5) make caches or search or messaging or observability tenant-aware; (6) build automated onboarding or export or deletion; (7) test cross-tenant isolation exhaustively; (8) plan for scale (sharding or cells) before you need it.

674
00:56:37.199 --> 00:56:38.095
Part 9.

675
00:56:38.375 --> 00:56:39.783
Summary and Practice.

676
00:56:41.033 --> 00:56:42.121
Key takeaways.

677
00:56:42.401 --> 00:56:53.835
Isolation is the whole game, and the tension is always sharing (cheap) versus dedicating (isolated), decided per tenant tier (§2.1, §2.2).

678
00:56:54.115 --> 00:56:55.993
Don't rely on query discipline.

679
00:56:56.273 --> 00:57:04.657
Enforce data isolation in the engine (RLS) or the topology (silo); a forgotten filter must not leak (L1, L2).

680
00:57:04.937 --> 00:57:15.646
Connect as a limited role for RLS to apply, set the tenant from the authenticated principal, and reset it per request on pooled connections (L2, Q13).

681
00:57:15.926 --> 00:57:24.097
Fence performance per tenant, concurrency caps, fair queuing, rate limits, so no tenant breaches another's SLO (L3, L4).

682
00:57:25.447 --> 00:57:33.703
Plans, limits, and metering are how SaaS monetizes and protects itself; enforce limits centrally and atomically (L4).

683
00:57:33.983 --> 00:57:41.748
Make every subsystem tenant-aware, DB, cache, search, messaging, observability, config (§2.11).

684
00:57:42.028 --> 00:57:51.415
Automate the lifecycle, onboarding, export, complete deletion (crypto-shred), and respect residency (§2.8, §2.9).

685
00:57:51.695 --> 00:57:58.799
Scale with sharding and cells at high tenant counts; pool the small, silo the whales (§2.10).

686
00:58:00.149 --> 00:58:01.471
Decision cheat sheet.

687
00:58:01.751 --> 00:58:03.095
Here is a quick table:

688
00:58:03.375 --> 00:58:04.698
Question, and Answer.

689
00:58:04.978 --> 00:58:07.666
Isolation for the long tail of small tenants:

690
00:58:07.946 --> 00:58:09.333
Pool + RLS.

691
00:58:09.613 --> 00:58:12.407
Isolation for large or regulated tenants:

692
00:58:12.687 --> 00:58:15.482
Silo (dedicated DB, possibly own cell).

693
00:58:15.762 --> 00:58:17.661
Where does the tenant id come from:

694
00:58:17.941 --> 00:58:21.439
The authenticated JWT, never request input.

695
00:58:21.719 --> 00:58:23.789
Backstop against a forgotten filter:

696
00:58:24.069 --> 00:58:26.330
Row-Level Security (fail closed).

697
00:58:26.610 --> 00:58:28.082
RLS not working?:

698
00:58:28.362 --> 00:58:31.797
You're connected as owner or superuser, use a limited role.

699
00:58:32.077 --> 00:58:34.082
Pooled connections + RLS:

700
00:58:34.362 --> 00:58:37.114
SET LOCAL app.tenant inside a transaction.

701
00:58:37.394 --> 00:58:38.461
Noisy neighbor:

702
00:58:38.741 --> 00:58:42.730
Concurrency caps + fair queuing + rate limits; silo the whale.

703
00:58:43.010 --> 00:58:44.354
Enforce plan limits:

704
00:58:44.634 --> 00:58:47.429
Per-tenant token bucket, atomic (Redis Lua).

705
00:58:48.779 --> 00:58:50.229
Complete tenant deletion:

706
00:58:50.509 --> 00:58:53.752
Automated delete across all stores + crypto-shred.

707
00:58:54.032 --> 00:58:55.632
100,000 tenants:

708
00:58:55.912 --> 00:58:58.493
Pool + RLS + sharding + cells.

709
00:58:59.843 --> 00:59:00.974
Exercises.

710
00:59:01.254 --> 00:59:12.305
(B2) Extend L2's RLS to add an INSERT policy that stamps tenantid from app.tenant automatically, and prove a tenant can't insert a row for another tenant.

711
00:59:12.585 --> 00:59:25.427
(B2) Reproduce the pooled-connection RLS leak (Q13): set app.tenant without SET LOCAL, reuse the connection for another tenant, and show the leak; then fix it with SET LOCAL.

712
00:59:25.707 --> 00:59:34.454
(B3) Add weighted fair queuing to L3 (enterprise gets 3 turns per round, free gets 1) and measure how each tier's wait changes.

713
00:59:35.804 --> 00:59:47.751
(B3) Extend L4 to add a monthly quota (a hard cap per tenant) alongside the rate limit, and return 429 versus 402 Payment Required appropriately.

714
00:59:48.031 --> 00:59:59.039
(B4) Design the tenant, then shard, then cell routing map for 100k tenants: what's in the control plane, how a request is routed, and how you rebalance a hot shard.

